/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A biosensor is an analytical device.
   - A plain chemical sensor is not a biosensor.
   - The chain is analyte → bioreceptor → transducer → signal conditioning → display.
   - Two classifications matter.
   - Second, by transducer: amperometric (current at fixed potential), potentiometric (voltage at near-zero current), conductometric (conductance), optical (absorbance or fluorescence), piezoelectric (mass shifts frequency) and calorimetric (hea
   - Enzyme biosensors come in three generations.
   - Desired characteristics: reliability (same sample, same answer), simplicity, cost in rupees per test, sensitivity, selectivity, dynamic range (lowest to highest usable concentration), response time (time to 90% of final reading), drift, and
   - A calibration curve plots signal against known concentration.
   - LOD is detection limit in concentration units; σ is standard deviation of blank readings in signal units; S is sensitivity, the calibration slope in signal units per concentration unit.
   - Matrix is everything except the analyte; it can suppress or enhance signal.
*/
