/* book: lead: A biosensor must pick one molecule out of a blood sample full of others, and the bio-recognition element does that picking. Enzymes catalyse one substrate, antibodies bind one antigen, nucleic acid probes pair with one complementary sequence. The element's kinetics or binding constant sets the sensor's range and detection limit. Glucometer strips, pregnancy tests and DNA test cards all rest on thi */
/* book Figure 4.2.1: Michaelis–Menten curves for the worked example: Vmax = 120, Km = 25 mmol/L in solution and an apparent Km of 60 mmol/L on the electrode. Each curve reaches half of Vmax at its own Km; the larger apparent Km keeps the response closer to a straight line over the blood range. */
