/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Most enzymes are protein catalysts; a few are RNA.
   - v is the rate (µmol min⁻¹ per mg of enzyme); Vmax is the rate when every site is occupied; S is substrate concentration; Km is the substrate concentration giving v = Vmax/2.
   - Km measures how tightly the enzyme holds its substrate: a small Km means tight binding and early saturation.
   - On a glucose electrode the enzyme reaction runs to completion by itself.
   - An antibody is not a catalyst.
   - θ is the fraction of antibody sites occupied; [Ag] is the free antigen concentration; Kd is the dissociation constant, in the same units.
   - A nucleic acid probe is a short single strand, for example a 20-base oligomer.
   - Immobilisation methods: adsorption is simple but leaks; covalent coupling is stable; gel entrapment holds whole cells and enzymes; self-assembled monolayers of thiols on gold give an ordered, reproducible surface.
*/
